NAME
       dd - copy data in blocks, between files and devices

SYNOPSIS
       dd if=INPUT of=OUTPUT [bs=SIZE] [count=N] [skip=N] [seek=N] [status=WHAT]

DESCRIPTION
       dd copies from INPUT to OUTPUT in blocks of a set size. It is used to
       make a file of a known size (for example for swap or a test), to copy
       or wipe the start of a disk, and to write images. Without if= it reads
       standard input; without of= it writes standard output. At the end it
       reports how much it copied.

       Be careful with of=: dd writes over a disk or partition without asking.

OPERANDS
       if=FILE         read from FILE
       of=FILE         write to FILE
       bs=SIZE         block size: 512, 4K, 1M, 1G (K, M, G are powers of 1024)
       count=N         copy only N blocks
       skip=N          skip N blocks of the input first
       seek=N          skip N blocks of the output first
       conv=notrunc    do not cut the output file short
       status=progress show progress while copying; status=none prints nothing

EXAMPLES
       Make a 100 MiB file of zeros:

           dd if=/dev/zero of=/root/test.img bs=1M count=100

       Wipe the partition table at the start of /dev/vdb (destroys its
       partitions):

           dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/vdb bs=1M count=1

SEE ALSO
       truncate(1), fallocate(1), wipefs(8), mkswap(8)
